rix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ by their respective norms, we obtain the second and third columns of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}_{1}}{‖{e}_{1}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\frac{{e}_{2}}{‖{e}_{2}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{5}}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{15} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{15} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186357732 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second and third columns of the matrix $P?$"] [17592186357732 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ So, the second and third columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ Computing $A-3I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors ${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are not orthogonal. So, we proceed using the Gram-Schmidt process. We set \n\n$$\n{e}_{2}={v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand we find ${e}_{3}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{3} & ={v}_{3}-\\frac{{v}_{3}\\cdot {e}_{2}}{{e}_{2}\\cdot {e}_{2}}{e}_{2} \\\\  & ={v}_{3}-\\frac{1}{5}{e}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing ${e}_{2}$ and ${e}_{3}$ by their respective norms, we obtain the second and third columns of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}_{2}}{‖{e}_{2}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\frac{{e}_{3}}{‖{e}_{3}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\frac{24}{5}}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{15} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{30}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186357778 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 9 \\\\ 8 & 11\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186357779 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186357789 :answer/value "P=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186357796 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 7 \\\